          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %em Perfectoid spaces
            are a variant of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Huber+spaces"} Huber spaces
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+geometry"} analytic geometry
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Scholze11"} Scholze 11
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#FontaineWinterberger79"} Fontaine-Winterberger 79
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+field+analogy"} function field analogy
          %p
            This theorem establishes an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            between the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absolute+Galois+groups"} absolute Galois groups
            of the extension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mo ∪
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi ℚ
                    %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %msup
                        %mi p
                        %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \coloneqq \cup_{n}\mathbb{Q}_{p}[p^{1/p^{n}}]
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-adic+numbers"} p-adic numbers
            and of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perfect+field"} perfection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi L
                    %mo ♭
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mo ∪
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔽
                    %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi t
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %msup
                        %mi p
                        %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^{\flat} \coloneqq \cup_{n}\mathbb{F}_{p}((t^{1/p^{n}}))
            of the field of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Laurent+series"} Laurent series
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+field"} finite field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔽
                    %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{F}_p
            (the isomorphism between Galois groups continues to hold after passing to the completions of these fields - it is these completions that are more properly called
            = succeed ")." do
              %em perfectoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi L
                    %mo ♭
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^{\flat}
            is called the
            %em tilt
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Scholze11"} Scholze 11
            %a{:href => "#Bhatt14"} Bhatt 14
            for a review).
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p
            The reference for these definitions are
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Weinstein17"} Weinstein17
          %h3#perfectoid_fields Perfectoid fields
          %ul
            %li
              A nonarchimedean field
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              of residue characteristic
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              is
              %em perfectoid
              if its value group is nondiscrete and the
              = succeed "-th" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi K
                      %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K^{\circ}/p
              is surjective (here
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi K
                      %mo ∘
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K^{\circ}
              is the subset of power bounded elements).
          %h3#perfectoid_rings Perfectoid rings
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A
                %em Huber ring
                is a topological ring
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                which contains an open subring
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_{0}
                which is adic with respect to a finitely generated ideal of definition
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi I
                        %mo ⊆
                        %msub
                          %mi A
                          %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I\subseteq A_{0}
            %li
              %p
                A Huber ring
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                is
                %em Tate
                if it contains a topologically nilpotent unit
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϖ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varpi
                (also called a
                = succeed ")." do
                  %em pseudouniformizer
            %li
              %p
                A Tate Huber ring is
                %em perfectoid
                if it is complete, uniform (the subset of power-bounded elements is bounded), and contains a pseudo-uniformizer
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϖ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varpi
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ϖ
                        %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varpi^{p}\vert p
                and the p-th power map
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi ϖ
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %msup
                        %mi ϖ
                        %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A/\varpi\to A/\varpi^{p}
                is an isomorphism.
          %h3#perfectoid_space Perfectoid space
          %ul
            %li
              A
              %em perfectoid space
              is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adic+space"} adic space
              which can be covered by affinoids
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Spa
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %msup
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spa(A,A^{+})
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is a perfectoid ring.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#tilting Tilting
          %p The concept of tilting allows us to generalize the theorem of Fontaine and Wintenberger on the isomorphism of the absolute Galois groups of a perfectoid field and its tilt mentioned above, to perfectoid rings, and further to perfectoid spaces.
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              (
              %a{:href => "#ScholzeWeinstein20"}> ScholzeWeinstein20
              , Definition 6.2.1) Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              be a perfectoid ring. The
              %em tilt
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              is
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi R
                      %mo ♭
                    %mo =
                    %munder
                      %mi lim
                      %munder
                        %mo ←
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo ↦
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mi p
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    R^{\flat}=\underset
                    {\underset{x\mapsto x^{p}}{\leftarrow}}
                    {\lim}R
            %p A priori this is a topological multiplicative monoid, so turn it into a topological ring we equip it with the addition structure given by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mi y
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %munder
                      %mi lim
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo →
                        %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi i
                        %mo +
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                    %msup
                      %mi y
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi i
                        %mo +
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi p
                          %mi n
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x+y)^{(i)}=\lim_{n\to\infty}(x^{(i+n)}+y^{(i+n)})^{p^{n}}.
            %p
          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              (
              %a{:href => "#ScholzeWeinstein20"}> ScholzeWeinstein20
              , Theorem 7.4.5)
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              be a perfectoid ring and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi R
                      %mo ♭
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R^{\flat}
              be its tilt.
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  For any finite etale
                  = succeed "-algebra" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi R
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi S
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                  is perfectoid.
              %li
                %p
                  Tilting
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                        %mo ↦
                        %msup
                          %mi S
                          %mo ♭
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S\mapsto S^{\flat}
                  induces an equivalence between finite etale
                  = succeed "-algebras" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi R
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                  = succeed "-algebras." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi R
                            %mo ♭
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R^{\flat}
              %li
                %p
                  For any finite etale
                  = succeed "-algebra" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi R
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi S
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi S
                            %mo ∘
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^{\circ}
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi R
                            %mo ∘
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R^{\circ}
            %p
          %p
            The tilting construction “glues” and therefore carries over from perfectoid rings to perfectoid spaces; in other words to a perfectoid space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            we can take the tilt to obtain a perfectoid space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mo ♭
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{\flat}
            of characteristic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            (
            %a{:href => "#ScholzeWeinstein20"}> ScholzeWeinstein20
            , 7.1).
          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              (
              %a{:href => "#ScholzeWeinstein20"}> ScholzeWeinstein20
              , Corollary 7.5.3)
            %p
              There exists an etale site
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi et
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_{et}
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi et
                    %mo ≅
                    %msubsup
                      %mi X
                      %mi et
                      %mo ♭
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_{et}\cong X_{et}^{\flat}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi H
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi et
                    %mo ,
                    %msubsup
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^{i}(X_{et},\mathcal{O}_{X}^{+})
              is almost zero for all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\geq 1
              and for all affinoids
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %p
          %h2#generalizations Generalizations
          %p
            The concept of perfectoid space can be generalized into that of a
            = succeed "," do
              %em diamond
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            by a perfectoid equivalence relation (
            %a{:href => "#ScholzeWeinstein20"}> ScholzeWeinstein20
            , Definition 8.3.1). The concept of diamond can similarly be generalized into that of a
            = succeed "," do
              %em v-sheaf
            %em small v-sheaf
            is a quotient of a diamond by a diamond equivalence relation (
            %a{:href => "#ScholzeWeinstein20"}> ScholzeWeinstein20
            , Proposition 17.2.2).
          %h2#applications Applications
          %p
            One application of perfectoid spaces is in relating
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+representations"} Galois representations
            to torsion in the cohomology of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Shimura+varieties"} Shimura varieties
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperbolic+3-space"} hyperbolic 3-space
            by an arithmetic subgroup). This is surveyed in section 5 of
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Weinstein15"} Weinstein15
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#CaraianiIHES"} CaraianiIHES
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modularity+theorem"} modularity theorem
            for elliptic curves over quadratic imaginary fields.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berkovich+space"} Berkovich space
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+analytic+geometry"} global analytic geometry
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-adic+Hodge+theory"} p-adic Hodge theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perfectoid+field"} perfectoid field
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prismatic+cohomology"} prismatic cohomology
          %h2#references References
          %p Exposition includes
          %ul
            %li
              Michael Harris,
              %em The perfectoid concept: Test case for an absent theory
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.columbia.edu/~harris/otherarticles_files/perfectoid.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p The concept is due to
          %ul
            %li#Scholze11
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Scholze"}> Peter Scholze
              ,
              %em Perfectoid spaces
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1111.4914"}> arXiv:1111.4914
              )
          %p motivated by
          %ul
            %li#FontaineWinterberger79
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Marc+Fontaine"}> Jean-Marc Fontaine
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Pierre+Wintenberger"} Jean-Pierre Wintenberger
              = succeed "," do
                %em Extensions algébrique et corps des normes des extensions APF des corps locaux
          %p Review includes
          %ul
            %li#Bhatt14
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bhargav+Bhatt"}> Bhargav Bhatt
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em What is… a perfectoid space?
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/BhattPerfectoidSpace.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bhargav+Bhatt"}> Bhargav Bhatt
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Lecture notes for a class on perfectoid spaces
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www-personal.umich.edu/~bhattb/teaching/mat679w17/lectures.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Scholze"}> Peter Scholze
                ,
                %em Perfectoid spaces: a survey
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1303.5948"}> arXiv:1303.5948
                )
            %li#Weinstein17
              %p
                Jared Weinstein,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Adic spaces
                %a{:href => "https://swc-math.github.io/aws/2017/2017WeinsteinNotes.pdf"} pdf
          %p Perfectoid spaces and related concepts were the topic of a course at Berkeley in 2014, whose lecture notes have now been made into a book:
          %ul
            %li#ScholzeWeinstein20
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Scholze"} Peter Scholze
              and Jared Weinstein,
              %em Berkeley Lectures on p-adic Geometry
              %a{:href => "https://www.math.uni-bonn.de/people/scholze/Berkeley.pdf"} pdf
          %p Some applications of perfectoid spaces are discussed in
          %ul
            %li#Weinstein15
              Jared Weinstein,
              %em Reciprocity Laws and Galois Representations: Recent Breakthroughs
              %a{:href => "https://math.bu.edu/people/jsweinst/CEB/BAMS.pdf"} pdf
          %p Progress in the theory is being applied to prove a modularity theorem for elliptic curves over quadratic imaginary fields, discussed in
          %ul
            %li#CaraianiIHES
              Ana Caraiani,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Modularity over CM Fields
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MM6sebSdtCc"} YouTube
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia,
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perfectoid_space"} Perfectoid space
          %p
            Formalization in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            (in
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lean"} Lean
          %ul
            %li#BCM
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kevin+Buzzard"}> Kevin Buzzard
              , Johan Commelin, Patrick Massot,
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://github.com/leanprover-community/lean-perfectoid-spaces"} lean-perfectoid-spaces
        .revisedby
